    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola",
    "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9}-(x+4)^{2}=1?$",
    "worked_solution": "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{1}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-4$ and $k=7.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-4,7).$",
